\caption(The selected measurements and target composition. The lower-left region, including equality at eight, is negative; crossing either cutoff is positive. Many images share the same integer coordinate.)
\alttext(A scatter of integer pixel intensities with horizontal and vertical cutoff lines at eight. Observations occupy several quadrants, and multiple images overlap at the same position.)

Other pixels are irrelevant to the definition of this target but remain
correlated measurements from real images. They are not independent random
noise. We reserve 30% of the rows with the seeded split below, leaving
1258 training and 539 test images. Encoding uses the same fixed cutoff and
rule on both partitions, with no fitted preprocessing parameters.

> The method: mistake-driven multiplication

With \(d\) Boolean features, initialise \(w_j=1\), choose threshold
\(\theta=d\), and predict

$$
\widehat y=\mathbf1\!\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{d}w_jx_j>\theta\right\},\qquad
w_j\leftarrow
\begin{cases}
2w_j & x_j=1,\ \widehat y=0,\ y=1,\\
w_j/2 & x_j=1,\ \widehat y=1,\ y=0,\\
w_j & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(Predict one only when the active-weight sum strictly exceeds the threshold. Double active weights on a false negative, halve them on a false positive, and otherwise leave them unchanged.)

A score exactly equal to the threshold predicts zero. The library allows
other finite update factors greater than one and other nonnegative
thresholds; the following bound is for factor two and threshold d. A
complete pass with no online mistakes sets `converged=true`. That certifies
classification of the supplied training rows by the unchanged weights,
not agreement with the target on every possible input.

>> A potential argument for logarithmic dependence on dimension

Assume exact arithmetic and labels from a fixed monotone OR of \(k\)
relevant Boolean attributes among \(d\ge1\). Let \(P\) and \(F\)
be the numbers of false negatives and false positives in any presented
sequence, including repeated passes.

On a negative example all relevant attributes are zero, so relevant
weights are never demoted. Every false negative activates at least one
relevant attribute and promotes it. Just before that update its weight is
at most the score, which is at most \(d\); afterwards it is at most
\(2d\). Thus the sum of base-two logarithms of relevant weights starts
at zero, increases by at least one per false negative, and cannot exceed
\(k\log_2(2d)\):

$$
P\le k\log_2(2d).
$$
\alttext(The number of false-negative promotions is bounded by k times the base-two logarithm of twice the total number of attributes.)

Now use total weight \(W=\sum_j w_j\), initially \(d\). A false
negative increases it by the active score, at most \(d\). A false
positive decreases it by half the active score, more than \(d/2\).
Since total weight stays positive,

$$
0<W_{\mathrm{final}}\le d+dP-\frac d2F,
\qquad F<2+2P,
\qquad M=P+F<2+3k\log_2(2d).
$$
\alttext(Positivity of the total weight bounds false-positive demotions by twice the false-negative promotions plus two. Combining the two bounds gives logarithmic dimension dependence for total online mistakes.)

This simple bound is sufficient for the experiment, rather than a claim
of the sharpest constants in Littlestone's analysis. It does not assume
independence between attributes. Irrelevant attributes can be promoted on
positive examples; their individual promotions and demotions need not
cancel. The potential controls the aggregate behaviour instead.

> The actual training trace and a frozen held-out evaluation

The DataFrame adapter validates Boolean values and calls the same update
loop as `winnow_train()`. Its trace distinguishes online mistakes during
a pass from accuracy of the final weights after that pass. These can
differ: the last update in a pass may already fix every training row,
while a later zero-mistake pass is needed to confirm convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/winnow_passes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits(); auto predictors = digits.columns();
  predictors.erase(std::remove(predictors.begin(),predictors.end(),"digit"),predictors.end());
  const auto [train,test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(digits,0.3,42);
  const auto training = dm::algorithms::winnow_disjunction_data(train,predictors,{"pixel_27","pixel_28"});
  const auto fit = dm::algorithms::winnow_fit(training);
  std::print("{}\n",fit.history.to_md(6));
  std::print("\nTraining:\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::winnow_evaluate(fit,training).to_md(6));
  std::print("\nHeld-out images:\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::winnow_evaluate(fit,
      dm::algorithms::winnow_disjunction_data(test,predictors,{"pixel_27","pixel_28"})).to_md(6));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(fit.history.get_column("epoch").values(),fit.history.get_column("mistakes").values(),
      "online mistakes",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .line(fit.history.get_column("epoch").values(),fit.history.get_column("cumulative_mistakes").values(),
          "cumulative",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Winnow: the first pass learns; the second confirms no updates")
      .x_label("completed pass").y_label("online updates").size(1000,480).save_svg("build/winnow_passes.svg");
}
```
