| probe | score | expected | predicted | correct |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| zero | 0.000 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| only pixel_27 | 64.000 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| only pixel_28 | 64.000 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| other features on | 104.875 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| relevant features on | 128.000 | 1 | 1 | 1 |

\image(figures/winnow_weights.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The largest final weights and synthetic Boolean probes. The horizontal line marks log2(threshold). A single active weight must lie strictly above that line to predict one.)
\alttext(A plot of all sixty-four log weights, with the two target weights highlighted at the threshold line and other attributes spread above and below their initial unit weight.)

Both relevant weights are 64, exactly equal to the threshold. A Boolean
vector activating only one of them has target one but is predicted zero.
Activating all other attributes while both relevant ones are zero gives
score 104.875, above 64, producing a false positive for the specified OR.
These probes are valid Boolean vectors but need not resemble real digit
images. They demonstrate that perfect observed accuracy has not identified
the target function on its entire Boolean domain. If such examples were
presented for further learning, Winnow would update again; the reported
probes deliberately freeze the model.

> Adding attributes while keeping the target fixed

The next experiment orders the two relevant pixels first and then adds
other pixel columns. Each fit uses the same training rows, row order,
cutoff, target and update factor. Its threshold is the selected dimension.
The test data remain the same reserved images. Changing the dimension
changes the algorithm as specified; this is a controlled descriptive
experiment, not a search for a best test-set configuration.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/winnow_dimensions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train,test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::digits(),0.3,42);
  std::vector<std::string> order{"pixel_27","pixel_28"};
  for (int j=0;j<64;++j) if (j!=27 && j!=28) order.push_back(std::format("pixel_{}",j));
  std::vector<double> log_dimension,mistakes,bound;
  std::print("| attributes | online mistakes | passes | test mistakes | test accuracy | bound (strict) |\n"
      "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const std::size_t d : {2,8,16,32,64}) {
    const std::vector<std::string> predictors(order.begin(),order.begin()+d);
    const auto fit = dm::algorithms::winnow_fit(dm::algorithms::winnow_disjunction_data(train,predictors,{"pixel_27","pixel_28"}));
    const auto score = dm::algorithms::winnow_evaluate(fit,dm::algorithms::winnow_disjunction_data(test,predictors,{"pixel_27","pixel_28"}));
    log_dimension.push_back(std::log2(d)); mistakes.push_back(fit.model.total_mistakes); bound.push_back(2+6*std::log2(2*d));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",d,fit.model.total_mistakes,fit.model.epochs_run,
        score.double_at("mistakes",0),score.double_at("accuracy",0),bound.back());
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(log_dimension,mistakes,"observed updates",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .line(log_dimension,bound,"mistake bound",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("More attributes: observed updates stay below a logarithmic bound")
      .x_label("log2(number of attributes)").y_label("online updates")
      .size(1000,480).save_svg("build/winnow_dimensions.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| attributes | online mistakes | passes | test mistakes | test accuracy | bound (strict) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1.000000 | 14.000000 |
| 8 | 8 | 2 | 0 | 1.000000 | 26.000000 |
| 16 | 9 | 2 | 0 | 1.000000 | 32.000000 |
| 32 | 13 | 2 | 0 | 1.000000 | 38.000000 |
| 64 | 15 | 2 | 0 | 1.000000 | 44.000000 |
\image(figures/winnow_dimensions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Observed online update counts alongside the derived upper bound, with the dimension axis on a base-two logarithmic scale. The bound is not an expected learning curve or a bound on a frozen model's test error.)
\alttext(A dimension-sensitivity chart with observed update counts well below a higher straight reference line representing the logarithmic mistake bound.)

This bound depends on Boolean inputs, a consistent monotone-disjunction
target and the stated update/threshold choices. Arbitrary noisy labels or
continuous feature values do not inherit it. Training visits every
attribute of every presented row, so logarithmic mistake dependence does
not mean logarithmic runtime: at most \(O(End)\) work for \(E\) passes
over \(n\) rows and \(d\) attributes. The core stores \(O(d)\) weights;
the DataFrame adapter additionally materialises \(O(nd)\) Boolean data
and \(O(E)\) history. It rejects invalid shapes/values and reports
floating-point overflow or positive-weight underflow instead of silently
changing the mathematical rule.

> Conclusion

A potential argument explains why a sparse realizable OR can be learned
with only logarithmic dependence of online mistakes on total dimension.
On thresholded image measurements, the shared Winnow loop needs fifteen
updates and gets every observed held-out target right. Its learned weights
still fail simple out-of-support Boolean probes. The experiment therefore
separates mistake bounds during learning, accuracy on observed data, and
identification of a function over its entire input domain.

> References

- N. Littlestone (1988). *Learning Quickly When Irrelevant Attributes Abound:
  A New Linear-threshold Algorithm*. Machine Learning 2, 285–318.
  https://ai.stanford.edu/~pabbeel/depth_qual/littlestone1988.pdf
- scikit-learn, *Optical recognition of handwritten digits dataset*:
  https://scikit-learn.org/stable/datasets/toy_dataset.html#optical-recognition-of-handwritten-digits-dataset
